---
// Lesson 9.2 — Hex, RGB & Data Abstraction
// expand-app: hexadecimal, Color32 as 4 bytes, RGBA in Inspector.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>9.2 — Hex, RGB &amp; Data Abstraction</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You have clicked colors in Unity a hundred times. Behind that
    picker is the exact place where binary becomes USEFUL: colors are
    bytes, written in hex, and the hex is just binary with better
    handwriting. Today you read the Inspector's secret language.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Hex = binary in groups of 4.</strong> One hex digit
    (0–9, A–F) covers exactly 4 bits: 0000=0 … 1111=F. A byte is
    therefore exactly TWO hex digits. Nobody reads
    1111111111111111 when FF FF says the same thing out loud.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Dec</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>8</td><td>9</td><td>10</td><td>11</td><td>12</td><td>13</td><td>14</td><td>15</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hex</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>8</td><td>9</td><td>A</td><td>B</td><td>C</td><td>D</td><td>E</td><td>F</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Bin</th><td>0000</td><td>0001</td><td>0010</td><td>0011</td><td>0100</td><td>0101</td><td>0110</td><td>0111</td><td>1000</td><td>1001</td><td>1010</td><td>1011</td><td>1100</td><td>1101</td><td>1110</td><td>1111</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>A color is four bytes.</strong> Unity's
    <code>Color32</code> is R, G, B, A — one byte each, 0–255, so a
    color is literally a hex number like <code>FF6600FF</code>
    (full red, strong orange-green mix, no blue, full alpha). When
    you type <code>new Color32(255, 102, 0, 255)</code> you are
    writing <code>FF6600FF</code> in decimal cosplay:</p>
    <pre><code>// The site you are reading right now is themed blue:
// #6cb2ff → R=6c=108, G=b2=178, B=ff=255  → (108, 178, 255)</code></pre>
    <p>That IS your theme color, decoded by hand. The # in CSS means
    "hex follows," and web colors are 6 hex digits because web alpha
    is separate.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The exam loves this: "how many colors can RGB represent?"
            Work it — 256 per channel, three channels — and say WHY
            the answer is 256³ (that is <a href="9.1.html">9.1</a>'s
            bits-to-range law, cubed). Then: why is 4 bits per
            channel (like old consoles) only 4096 colors — what did
            8-bit color BUY?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — decode your game's palette.</strong> Pick
    three colors from your sprites/UI (use the eyedropper in the
    Sprite Editor or any color picker). For each, write the hex AND
    the decimal triple. Your cave should have a palette story — dark
    rock (low RGB values), bright coin (high R and G) — and now you
    can QUOTE it.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — program a palette.</strong> Colors as data —
    the abstraction, in code:</p>
    <pre><code>public static class Palette
{
    //              name        hex        Color32
    public static readonly Color32 CAVE_ROCK = new Color32(0x4a, 0x4a, 0x55, 0xff);
    public static readonly Color32 COIN_GOLD = new Color32(0xff, 0xc4, 0x3d, 0xff);
    public static readonly Color32 DANGER    = new Color32(0xff, 0x33, 0x33, 0xff);
}

// your 8.3 hit flash, upgraded:
sr.color = new Color32(0xff, 0xff, 0xff, 0xff); // white for 0.1s
// phase 3 boss tint:
sr.color = Palette.DANGER;</code></pre>
    <p>Why a palette class beats per-object color picking: change ONE
    line, the whole game's mood shifts. That is
    <strong>abstraction</strong> — the exam's word, in your repo.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the alpha byte.</strong> The fourth byte is
    transparency. <code>0xff</code> = solid; <code>0x80</code> =
    half-visible (your hit flash could EASE from 0x80 to 0xff).
    Change your shockwave prefab's sprite alpha to 0x80 and note the
    readability change: transparent threats read as "avoidable" —
    alpha is communication, not decoration.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Byte bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>new Color32(300, 0, 0, 255) — what happens to the
                300? (Bytes CLAMP or wrap — which one, and why is
                silent clamping a design risk?)</li>
            <li>0x1F vs 0xF1 — a transposed hex pair. What color
                difference did that make? Why is hex the FASTEST
                way to catch a digit-swap bug in colors?</li>
            <li>Compute: how many bytes in a 1080p frame of RGBA
                data? (1920×1080×4 — do it on paper. Then ask: why
                does your GPU do this 60 times a second? That
                arithmetic explains why <a href="9.7.html">9.7</a>
                needs a graphics pipeline.)</li>
            <li>The exam's classic: a 16-bit color system splits
                5/6/5. Why does GREEN get the extra bit — and why
                do human eyes barely notice (look at your palette:
                which channel did you lean on?)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Palette design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design a DANGER palette shift for your boss phases:
                phase 1 neutral, phase 2 warm, phase 3 red-shifted.
                Write the three hex codes BEFORE you look at them —
                then check the feel.</li>
            <li>AP practice: "A game represents a color as 3 bits —
                one per channel. How many colors?" Answer AND
                reason — then tell your neighbor why old-school
                games looked "chunky."</li>
            <li>Hard: your own Color32 parser — read a string like
                "FF6600" with int.Parse(s, NumberStyles.HexNumber).
                Which two lines of code does this save in a
                data-driven palette file?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Convert hex ↔ binary ↔ decimal for bytes, fast</li>
            <li>Explain a color as four bytes and decode #RRGGBB by hand</li>
            <li>Build a palette class as a data abstraction</li>
            <li>Do the 256³ color-count argument from bits</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

